    "result_text": "Done. The test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe change derives a new read-only `locality` field on an owner from its city, using the fixed table Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD, returning `UNKNOWN` for any other city.\n\nThree edits:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and it's serialized on `GET /api/owners/{id}`).\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/LocalityResolver.java`** (new) \u2014 a small utility with a static `deriveLocality(String city)` implementing the city\u2192region table.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping` expression populating `locality` from the city via `LocalityResolver.deriveLocality(...)`.\n\n## A subtlety worth flagging\n\nMy first attempt put `deriveLocality` as a `default` method *inside* the MapStruct `OwnerMapper` interface. MapStruct then treated it as an implicit `String \u2192 String` conversion and silently applied it to **every** string property on both mapping directions \u2014 so on create, `toOwner(...)` mapped `telephone`, `email`, `city`, etc. all through `deriveLocality`, turning telephone/email into `\"UNKNOWN\"` and blowing up owner creation with a persist-time `ConstraintViolationException` (invalid E.164 telephone and malformed email).\n\nMoving the helper into a separate non-mapper class (`LocalityResolver`) and calling it via a fully-qualified expression stops MapStruct from adopting it as a mapping method, so only `locality` uses it.\n\nResults: `AcceptanceTest` (both `coreDerivesRegionForKnownCity` \u2192 `NSW` and `functionalityUnknownCityIsUnknown` \u2192 `UNKNOWN`) passes, and the full `mvn test` run is `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
